<%#
  A batch code, as the identifier it is.

  LNIKB02 is unique, it is what appears on an invoice and a
  certificate, and it is what somebody says out loud when they mean one
  particular run of a course. Printed as plain grey text among the dates
  it was the least noticeable thing in the line, and the hardest to
  pick out when three batches are listed together.

  So it is a pill, in a tabular face, and — wherever a batch id is
  known — a link that filters the schedule to it. The same code in the
  same shape on every page a teacher uses, which is what makes it
  recognisable at a glance rather than something to read.

  Expects: code. Optionally id (makes it a filter link) and href (to
  override where it goes).
%>
<% if (typeof code !== "undefined" && code) { %>
  <%
    const to = typeof href !== "undefined" && href
      ? href
      : (typeof id !== "undefined" && id ? "/teach/schedule?batch=" + id : null);
  %>
  <% if (to) { %>
    <a class="pill pill-code" href="<%= to %>" title="Show only this batch"><%= code %></a>
  <% } else { %>
    <%# No id, so nothing to filter to. Still a pill, because the point
        is that the code looks the same everywhere. %>
    <span class="pill pill-code"><%= code %></span>
  <% } %>
<% } %>
